// 5 · art-interview — In Conversation (A4; hanging Q/A markers, margin notes, speaker card)
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
const IV = {
  head: 'Nobody told me a song could be a room you live in.',
  deck: 'The composer Marisol Quint on writing for choirs of strangers, failing in public and the long afterlife of a lullaby.',
  qa: [
    ['You wrote your first piece for a choir that had never sung together. What did you learn that night?', 'That a room full of strangers will forgive almost anything except a lack of nerve. I had written something very careful, and it died on the first page. Then a woman in the back row, a retired bus driver, started improvising a drone under the second verse and the whole thing woke up. I have been trying to write for her ever since.'],
    ['Is that why your scores leave so much space?', 'Space is the point. A score that tells everyone exactly what to do is a set of instructions, and nobody sings instructions. If I leave a bar open, somebody will fill it with something I would never have thought of, and sometimes it is wrong, and sometimes the wrong thing is the best moment in the piece.'],
    ['You have said you distrust the word “inclusive.”', 'I distrust it as a decoration. Inclusion is not a poster in the foyer; it is whether the person with the weakest voice can find a part that is actually necessary. If their line can be cut without anyone noticing, you have not included them. You have tolerated them.'],
    ['What does failing in public teach you that failing in private does not?', 'Private failure is a draft. Public failure is a rehearsal for being forgiven. The first time a piece of mine fell apart in front of four hundred people, the audience laughed, and then they sang louder to carry us through. I have never forgotten that. It rearranged my idea of what an audience is for.'],
    ['How do you teach a choir a part by ear?', 'Slowly, and with food. I give them the shape before the notes: a hill, a staircase, a sigh. Then the notes arrive as details of a thing they already feel. People remember what a phrase does long before they remember what it is.'],
    ['Your lullaby “Small Weather” has been sung at weddings, funerals, once at a football match. How does it feel to lose control of a song?', 'Like watching a child leave home and become better at things than you are. I get letters. A man sang it to his dying father in a language I do not speak. I would be a fool to want that back.'],
    ['What are you afraid of now?', 'Tidiness. Success makes you tidy: better venues, better microphones, better-behaved audiences. I keep a list of things I am not allowed to fix.'],
    ['And what is on that list?', 'The cough in the second movement. The key change that nobody likes. The bar where the altos always come in early. They are all, I think, where the piece is alive.'],
    ['Do you ever write alone anymore?', 'Rarely, and I resent it. The best ideas arrive when someone says “I can’t reach that note.” Then you have to decide whether to move the note or move the person, and I always, eventually, move the note.'],
    ['What do you listen to when you are not working?', 'Silence, mostly, and the radio on the wrong station. I like the moment when two broadcasts bleed into each other and make a chord nobody intended. I have stolen half my harmony from interference.'],
    ['You teach as well. What do students get wrong?', 'They treat nerves as a problem to be solved. Nerves are information. They tell you that something is at stake. A choir with no nerves is a choir that has stopped listening to itself.'],
    ['How do you know when a piece is finished?', 'When someone I love has stopped asking me to change it, and I have stopped wanting to. Those two events rarely coincide. Usually I run out of time, and the performance decides for me.'],
    ['What would you tell the composer you were at twenty-five?', 'Write the dull parts first. Everyone waits to write the climax and then discovers the climax needs a road. Also, buy a better coat. You will be standing in a lot of cold church halls.'],
    ['Last question. What is a good song for?', 'To hold the door open. Somebody has to carry the weight of what we cannot say at the dinner table, and a three-minute song with a decent tune will do it without making a speech.'],
  ] as Array<[string, string]>,
  quick: [['Last book finished', 'A field guide to British lichens'], ['Song you wish you had written', '“Wayfaring Stranger”'], ['Warm-up you swear by', 'Humming into a cup of tea'], ['Instrument you cannot play', 'Anything with a reed'], ['Best advice you ignored', '“Write what you know.”'], ['Hidden talent', 'Parallel parking, any vehicle']],
  pull: '“A score that tells everyone exactly what to do is a set of instructions, and nobody sings instructions.”',
};

