    'Wages did not stand still. They rose faster than at any point since the early 2000s. But they started from a lower base, and rent is a bill that arrives on the first of every month regardless of when a pay rise lands.',
    'The bottom of the table is just as instructive. In the nine cities where wages beat rents, the common feature was supply: each permitted more than twice as many homes per thousand residents as the national median over the period.',
